/// \defgroup grp_w1_wwlib_targa_bufffile WWLib Targa image I/O and BufferedFileClass
/// \brief Two small WWLib file utilities: the Targa (.tga) image reader and
///        writer, and BufferedFileClass, a RawFileClass with a read-ahead
///        buffer.
///
/// Both come from Westwood's WWLib, the foundation library under the W3D
/// renderer; \ref sub_wwlib_thirdparty describes the library as a whole.
/// Both sit on WWLib's file abstraction: FileClass objects handed out by the
/// global file factories _TheFileFactory (reading) and
/// _TheWritingFileFactory (writing).
///
/// - Targa reads and writes Truevision TGA images, including the TGA 2.0
///   footer and extension area. In retail BFME 2 its only named caller
///   outside the class is WW3D::Make_Screen_Shot, which writes screenshots
///   as uncompressed 24-bit TGA files.
/// - BufferedFileClass is what SimpleFileFactoryClass, the default WWLib
///   read factory, hands out for a file name. It reads 16 KB at a time and
///   serves small reads from memory.
///
/// Both units are Zero Hour's WWLib sources. The BFME 2 differences are
/// small and are noted on Targa::YFlip and BufferedFileClass::Read.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - In retail BFME 2 nothing outside the class calls either Targa::Load
///   overload or Targa::Open. Whatever reads the .tga files a mod ships, it
///   is not this class, which in the game only writes screenshots.
/// - BufferedFileClass derives from RawFileClass, which opens a loose file by
///   name. It does not look inside BIG archives. In the game, archive-aware
///   access goes through W3DFileSystem, which installs itself as
///   _TheFileFactory when it is constructed.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The file layer is the seam, but only for reads. Reads go through the
///   virtual FileFactoryClass interface of _TheFileFactory, so replacing that
///   factory redirects them. Writes do not. _TheWritingFileFactory is a
///   RawFileFactoryClass pointer whose Get_File is non-virtual, so
///   File_Open_Write and File_Open_ReadWrite always create a RawFileClass,
///   which opens with CreateFileW on Windows. Redirecting screenshot output
///   means changing RawFileFactoryClass or RawFileClass, not swapping the
///   factory.
/// - The TGA structures are read and written as raw memory images of
///   byte-packed structs. That assumes a little-endian host, and also a
///   4-byte long for the footer and extension fields. A 64-bit build where
///   long is 8 bytes (LP64, as on Linux and macOS) would misread them.
/// - TGAHeader declares Width and Height as signed 16-bit fields, so a side
///   longer than 32767 pixels reads as negative and is not handled. Byte
///   counts are computed in 32-bit arithmetic, so very large images also
///   overflow before that limit.


/// \file TARGA.CPP
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_targa_bufffile
/// \brief The Targa class: TGA image load, save, flips and RLE coding.
///
/// Zero Hour's WWLib TARGA.CPP, compiled with the WWLib file classes: every
/// file access goes through a FileClass from the global file factories, not
/// through C runtime handles. The retail bodies confirm it: Targa::Close
/// returns the file to _TheFileFactory, and the private read helper forwards
/// to a FileClass virtual. The one BFME 2 change is Targa::YFlip. The file
/// also defines Targa_Error_Handler, which logs a message for a Targa error
/// code when debug logging is compiled in. In a release build it only
/// returns the code. The ledger has no retail row for it, and it is not
/// covered here.


/// \file bufffile.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_targa_bufffile
/// \brief BufferedFileClass: buffered reads on top of RawFileClass.
///
/// Zero Hour's WWLib bufffile.cpp. It defines the static desired buffer size
/// (16 KB by default) and the out-of-line members: the file-name
/// constructor, the destructor, Read, Write, Seek, Close and Reset_Buffer.
/// The default constructor lives in BufferedFileClassConstructor.cpp. The
/// copy-style constructor and operator= declared in bufffile.h are not
/// defined in either file. The read buffer is allocated with
/// scalar operator new and freed with scalar delete, as the retail calls
/// show. Zero Hour used the array forms.


/// \class Targa
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_targa_bufffile
/// \brief Reads and writes Truevision TGA images through WWLib's FileClass
///        layer.
///
/// A Targa object holds one image: the public 18-byte TGAHeader (Header),
/// an image buffer, an optional palette buffer, the TGA 2.0 extension area,
/// and at most one open file.
///
/// \par Ownership
/// The image and palette buffers are either owned or borrowed. A flag
/// records which. Targa::Load(const char *, long, bool) allocates owned
/// buffers with malloc. Targa::SetImage and Targa::SetPalette install
/// borrowed buffers. The destructor and both setters free only owned
/// buffers. GetImage and GetPalette return the current pointers; ownership
/// stays with the object.
///
/// \par Pixel layout after loading
/// Loading normalises the image so that it is stored bottom row first and
/// left to right: a file whose descriptor says right-to-left or top-to-bottom
/// is flipped, and the matching descriptor bit is cleared. Truecolor pixels
/// are byte-reversed unless the caller asks not to; see Targa::InvertImage.
/// Targa::Save writes the buffer in the same orientation and leaves it
/// unchanged.
///
/// \par Small public members
/// These carry no logic of their own:
/// - IsCompressed reports whether Header.ImageType is greater than 8, which
///   covers the RLE types.
/// - GetExtension returns the extension area that Targa::Open read, or null
///   when the file was not a TGA 2.0 file with an extension area.
///
/// \par Private helpers
/// This documentation is built with EXTRACT_PRIVATE=NO, so the private
/// members' own blocks are not rendered. In short:
/// - File_Read, File_Write and File_Seek forward to the open FileClass.
///   Nothing checks for null.
/// - Is_File_Open and Clear_File test and reset the file pointer.
/// - DecodeImage expands TGA run-length packets from the open file into the
///   image buffer. Its remaining-pixel count is unsigned, so a packet that
///   runs past the end of the image makes it write past the buffer until
///   the file runs out.
/// - EncodeImage writes the image as run-length packets: runs of up to 128
///   pixels, raw packets of up to 127. For the last pixel it reads one pixel
///   past the buffer, and when those bytes equal the last pixel the final
///   run is dropped. Only Save with TGAF_COMPRESS reaches it, and retail
///   never sets that flag.
/// - InvertImage reverses the byte order of every 3- or 4-byte pixel,
///   converting between TGA's blue-green-red order and red-green-blue in
///   memory. It leaves 8-bit and 16-bit images alone.
/// - File_Open_Read gets the file from _TheFileFactory. File_Open_Write and
///   File_Open_ReadWrite get it from _TheWritingFileFactory, through a
///   non-virtual call that always creates a RawFileClass.
///
/// \par Use in BFME 2
/// WW3D::Make_Screen_Shot fills Header for a 24-bit truecolor image and
/// installs its pixel buffer with SetImage. It flips the rows with YFlip and
/// writes the file with Save and TGAF_IMAGE: no compression, no palette, no
/// extension area. In the retail call graph, the other members are either
/// called only from inside the class (Open, the 4-argument Load, XFlip,
/// DecodeImage, EncodeImage, InvertImage) or not called at all (the flags
/// overload of Load, SetPalette, IsCompressed, GetExtension). Close and the
/// private File_Read, File_Write, File_Seek, Is_File_Open and Clear_File are
/// inlined where they are used, so their out-of-line copies have no caller
/// either. Zero Hour: the texture loader read .tga textures with
/// Targa::Open and the flags overload of Load.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The class supports color-mapped, truecolor and monochrome images, raw or
/// RLE (types 1, 2, 3, 9 and 10). It cannot load RLE monochrome (type 11),
/// although Save can write it. Since BFME 2 itself never loads through this
/// class, these limits matter only to code or tools that reuse it.
///
/// \see BufferedFileClass, \ref grp_w1_wwlib_targa_bufffile


/// \fn Targa::Targa(void)
/// \brief Creates an empty Targa: no file, no buffers, zeroed header and
///        extension area, read mode.
///
/// The buffers start as null and unowned, so a caller can install its own
/// with Targa::SetImage or Targa::SetPalette before saving or loading.
/// \see Targa::~Targa


/// \fn Targa::~Targa()
/// \brief Closes any open file and frees the image and palette buffers that
///        the object allocated.
///
/// Buffers installed by Targa::SetImage or Targa::SetPalette are not freed.
/// They stay the caller's responsibility. WW3D::Make_Screen_Shot relies on
/// this: it frees its own pixel buffer after the Targa object is gone.
/// \see Targa::Close


/// \fn long Targa::Open(const char *name, long mode)
/// \brief Opens a TGA file and, for reading, loads its header and leaves the
///        file positioned at the image data.
///
/// Targa::Load and Targa::Save call it. Any file that is already open is
/// closed first, unless it is open in the same mode.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - TGA_READMODE: opens through _TheFileFactory, then checks for a TGA 2.0
///   file. It reads the 26-byte footer at the end of the file; if the footer
///   has the "TRUEVISION-XFILE" signature and a nonzero extension offset, it
///   reads the extension area and marks the image as TGA 2.0. It then reads
///   the 18-byte header from the start of the file and skips the image ID
///   field. The stream is left at the color map, or at the pixels when
///   there is no color map.
/// - TGA_WRITEMODE: opens a file for writing through _TheWritingFileFactory.
///   Nothing is read or written.
/// - TGA_RDWRMODE: opens through _TheWritingFileFactory for reading and
///   writing, reads the header and skips the ID field. It does not look for
///   the footer.
/// - Any other mode opens nothing and returns 0.
/// - On any error the file is closed again.
///
/// \param name File name, resolved by the file factory for the mode.
/// \param mode TGA_READMODE, TGA_WRITEMODE or TGA_RDWRMODE.
/// \return 0 on success. TGAERR_OPEN if the file could not be opened (for
///         reading or read/write, also when the factory reports the file
///         unavailable). TGAERR_READ if a seek or read of the header, footer
///         or extension area failed.
///
/// \note If a file is already open in the requested mode, the function
///       returns 0 immediately and ignores name. Targa::Load(const char *,
///       long, bool) depends on this to keep reading the file it has open.
/// \see Targa::Close, Targa::Load(const char *, char *, char *, bool),
///      Targa::Save


/// \fn void Targa::Close(void)
/// \brief Closes the open file, if any, and hands it back to the file
///        factory.
///
/// The file always goes back to _TheFileFactory, including files that
/// Targa::Open got from _TheWritingFileFactory. That is safe because
/// W3DFileSystem, the game's read factory, and RawFileFactoryClass, the
/// writing factory, both dispose of a returned file by deleting it.
/// SimpleFileFactoryClass, the default read factory until W3DFileSystem
/// replaces it, does too in source; its retail body is not yet pinned.
/// In the source, Load, Save, Open's error path and the destructor call
/// it. In retail those calls are inlined, so this out-of-line copy has no
/// caller.
/// \see Targa::Open


/// \fn long Targa::Load(const char *name, char *palette, char *image, bool invert_image)
/// \brief Reads a TGA file's palette and pixels into the given buffers.
///
/// The worker behind Targa::Load(const char *, long, bool), and in retail
/// BFME 2 that overload is its only caller. It opens the file for reading
/// and closes it before returning.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Palette: for a color-mapped file, reads the color map into palette,
///   offset by the first-entry index from the header. When palette is null
///   or the map is empty, the color map is skipped.
/// - Pixels (only when image is not null): raw types 1, 2 and 3 are read
///   directly into image. RLE types 9 and 10 are decoded by
///   Targa::DecodeImage. Truecolor data (types 2 and 10) is passed to
///   Targa::InvertImage when invert_image is true. Any other type returns
///   TGAERR_NOTSUPPORTED.
/// - Orientation: if the header's X-origin bit is set, Targa::XFlip runs; if
///   the Y-origin (top) bit is set, Targa::YFlip runs. Each bit is cleared
///   after its flip, so the buffer and Header both describe a bottom-up,
///   left-to-right image.
///
/// \param name         File name, resolved through _TheFileFactory.
/// \param palette      Palette buffer, or null to skip the color map. It
///                     must hold the whole color map, including the entries
///                     before the first-entry index.
/// \param image        Pixel buffer of Width * Height * bytes-per-pixel
///                     bytes, or null to load only the palette.
/// \param invert_image Reverse the byte order of each truecolor pixel after
///                     reading; see Targa::InvertImage.
/// \return 0 on success. TGAERR_OPEN if the file could not be opened or its
///         header could not be read (Open's own error code is replaced).
///         TGAERR_READ for a short read. TGAERR_NOTSUPPORTED for an
///         unsupported image type.
///
/// \warning image is used only for the raw image types. RLE decoding,
///          the inversion and the flips all work on the object's own image
///          buffer, which Targa::SetImage or the flags overload installs. A
///          caller that passes a different buffer gets no pixels from an RLE
///          file and unflipped, uninverted pixels otherwise, while the
///          object's buffer is written instead (a crash if it has none). The
///          flags overload always passes the object's own buffer.
/// \see Targa::Load(const char *, long, bool), Targa::Open


/// \fn long Targa::Load(const char *name, long flags, bool invert_image)
/// \brief Loads a TGA file into buffers that the object allocates, or into
///        buffers the caller installed beforehand.
///
/// The convenient form of loading. It opens the file to read the header,
/// sizes the buffers from it, and then reads the data with
/// Targa::Load(const char *, char *, char *, bool) while the file is still
/// open. No retail BFME 2 code calls it. Zero Hour: the texture loader
/// called it with TGAF_IMAGE after installing its own palette buffer with
/// Targa::SetPalette.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - With TGAF_PAL and a color-mapped file, a palette the object owns is
///   freed, and a new one of color-map length times entry size is allocated
///   unless the caller installed one.
/// - With TGAF_IMAGE, an owned image buffer is freed and a new one of
///   Width * Height * bytes-per-pixel is allocated unless the caller
///   installed one. A caller-installed buffer is used as it is; its size
///   is not checked.
/// - Both buffers are then passed to the 4-argument Load. A caller-installed
///   palette is filled even when flags does not include TGAF_PAL.
///
/// \param name         File name, resolved through _TheFileFactory.
/// \param flags        TGAF_IMAGE and/or TGAF_PAL: which buffers to
///                     allocate.
/// \param invert_image Passed on; see Targa::InvertImage.
/// \return 0 on success. TGAERR_OPEN if the file could not be opened,
///         TGAERR_NOMEM if an allocation failed, otherwise the result of the
///         4-argument Load.
///
/// \warning The allocated palette covers only the color-map length, but the
///          4-argument Load writes it at an offset of the first-entry index.
///          A file whose color map does not start at entry 0 therefore
///          writes past the end of the allocated palette.
/// \see Targa::SetImage, Targa::SetPalette, Targa::GetExtension


/// \fn long Targa::Save(const char *name, long flags, bool addextension)
/// \brief Writes Header, the palette and the image buffer to a TGA 2.0 file.
///
/// The one Targa entry point that retail BFME 2 uses: WW3D::Make_Screen_Shot
/// calls it with TGAF_IMAGE to write each screenshot. The file is opened
/// through _TheWritingFileFactory and closed before returning.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Header is written with the ID length forced to 0. With TGAF_COMPRESS,
///   types 1, 2 and 3 are changed to their RLE forms 9, 10 and 11 in Header
///   itself (the change persists in the object). Types that are already
///   RLE stay as they are; for any other type compression is dropped.
/// - With TGAF_PAL, the palette entries from the first-entry index on are
///   copied to a temporary buffer and written.
/// - With TGAF_IMAGE, truecolor pixels are byte-reversed by
///   Targa::InvertImage, written raw or through Targa::EncodeImage, and
///   byte-reversed back, so the caller's buffer ends unchanged.
/// - With addextension, an extension area naming "Denzil's Targa Code" 1.00
///   is written, and the footer points to it.
/// - A TGA 2.0 footer always ends the file. Without an extension area its
///   extension offset is 0.
///
/// \param name         File name, resolved through _TheWritingFileFactory.
/// \param flags        TGAF_IMAGE, TGAF_PAL and TGAF_COMPRESS select what is
///                     written and how.
/// \param addextension Write the TGA 2.0 extension area.
/// \return 0 on success, TGAERR_OPEN, TGAERR_WRITE or TGAERR_NOMEM.
///
/// \note The result of Targa::EncodeImage is ignored, so a write error or
///       allocation failure during a compressed save is not reported.
///       Retail never saves compressed.
/// \see Targa::Open, Targa::EncodeImage


/// \fn void Targa::XFlip(void)
/// \brief Mirrors the image left to right in place.
///
/// Swaps each pixel of every row with its mirror pixel, one byte at a time.
/// Targa::Load(const char *, char *, char *, bool) calls it for files stored
/// right to left. It works on the object's image buffer and uses Header for
/// the size and pixel depth.
/// \see Targa::YFlip


/// \fn void Targa::YFlip(void)
/// \brief Mirrors the image top to bottom in place.
///
/// Swaps row i with row Height - 1 - i for the top half of the image.
/// Targa::Load(const char *, char *, char *, bool) calls it for files stored
/// top to bottom. WW3D::Make_Screen_Shot calls it on its top-down
/// screen copy so that Targa::Save writes the bottom-up rows a TGA file
/// expects by default.
///
/// \note BFME 2 swaps the rows one byte at a time, as BFME 1 does. Zero
///       Hour: the source keeps that loop only as a comment and swaps each
///       row pair with an inline-assembly helper instead. Open-BFME-1's copy
///       uses the byte loop too, matching BFME 1 retail.
/// \see Targa::XFlip


/// \fn char *Targa::SetImage(char *buffer)
/// \brief Installs a caller-owned image buffer.
///
/// Frees the current image buffer if the object owns it. The new buffer is
/// not owned: the destructor will not free it. WW3D::Make_Screen_Shot uses
/// it to hand its screen copy to the object before calling Targa::Save.
///
/// \param buffer The new image buffer, or null to detach the image.
/// \return The previous buffer if the caller had installed it, or null if
///         there was none or the object owned it (an owned buffer has
///         already been freed).
/// \see Targa::SetPalette


/// \fn char *Targa::SetPalette(char *buffer)
/// \brief Installs a caller-owned palette buffer.
///
/// The palette counterpart of Targa::SetImage, with the same ownership
/// rules. No retail BFME 2 code calls it.
///
/// \param buffer The new palette buffer, or null to detach the palette.
/// \return The previous caller-installed palette, or null.
/// \see Targa::SetImage


/// \fn long Targa::DecodeImage(void)
/// \brief Decodes TGA run-length data from the open file into the object's
///        image buffer.
///
/// Called by Targa::Load(const char *, char *, char *, bool) for image
/// types 9 and 10.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Each packet starts with a count byte. With the top bit set, the next
/// pixel is repeated (count & 0x7F) + 1 times; otherwise (count + 1) raw
/// pixels follow. Packets are read until Width * Height pixels have been
/// produced.
///
/// \return 0 on success, or TGAERR_READ if the file ended early.
///
/// \warning The remaining-pixel count is unsigned. A packet that runs past
///          the end of the image makes the count wrap around, and decoding then
///          continues until the file runs out, writing past the image
///          buffer. Only decode trusted files.
/// \see Targa::EncodeImage


/// \fn long Targa::EncodeImage(void)
/// \brief Writes the object's image buffer to the open file as TGA
///        run-length packets.
///
/// Called by Targa::Save when TGAF_COMPRESS is set. Retail never sets it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Compares each pixel with the start of the current packet. Repeats become
/// run packets of up to 128 pixels; other pixels collect in raw packets of
/// up to 127 pixels (the format allows 128). A packet buffer for the longest
/// packet is allocated with malloc for the call.
///
/// \return 0 on success, TGAERR_WRITE, or TGAERR_NOMEM if the packet buffer
///         could not be allocated.
///
/// \warning For the last pixel the comparison reads one pixel beyond the
///          image buffer. If those bytes happen to equal the last pixel, the
///          final run of equal pixels (at least the last pixel) is never
///          written and the file comes out short.
/// \see Targa::DecodeImage, Targa::Save


/// \fn void Targa::InvertImage(void)
/// \brief Reverses the byte order of every pixel in the object's image
///        buffer, for pixels of 3 or 4 bytes.
///
/// TGA stores truecolor pixels in blue, green, red (and alpha) byte order.
/// After inversion a 24-bit pixel reads red, green, blue in memory, and a
/// 32-bit pixel alpha, red, green, blue. 8-bit and 16-bit images are left
/// alone. Loading calls it when invert_image is true; Targa::Save calls it
/// twice around writing, so the file gets TGA byte order and the caller's
/// buffer is restored.
/// \see Targa::Load(const char *, char *, char *, bool), Targa::Save


/// \fn bool Targa::File_Open_Read(const char *name)
/// \brief Gets a file from _TheFileFactory and opens it for reading.
///
/// The file object is kept even when opening fails; Targa::Open's error
/// path then returns it to the factory through Targa::Close.
///
/// \param name File name.
/// \return true if the factory supplied a file, the file reports itself
///         available, and it opened.
/// \see Targa::Open


/// \fn bool Targa::File_Open_Write(const char *name)
/// \brief Gets a file from _TheWritingFileFactory and opens it for writing.
///
/// The file object is kept even when opening fails; Targa::Open's error
/// path then returns it to the factory through Targa::Close.
///
/// _TheWritingFileFactory is a RawFileFactoryClass, whose Get_File is not
/// virtual, so the file is always a new RawFileClass. In retail this
/// helper is inlined into Targa::Open.
///
/// \param name File name.
/// \return true if the factory supplied a file and it opened.
/// \see Targa::Open, Targa::Save


/// \fn bool Targa::File_Open_ReadWrite(const char *name)
/// \brief Gets a file from _TheWritingFileFactory and opens it for reading
///        and writing.
///
/// As in Targa::File_Open_Write, the factory call is not virtual, so the
/// file is always a new RawFileClass. The file object is kept even when
/// opening fails; Targa::Open's error path then returns it to the factory
/// through Targa::Close.
///
/// \param name File name.
/// \return true if the factory supplied a file, the file reports itself
///         available, and it opened.
/// \see Targa::Open


/// \class BufferedFileClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_targa_bufffile
/// \brief A RawFileClass that reads ahead into a 16 KB buffer, so that many
///        small reads cost few operating-system reads.
///
/// SimpleFileFactoryClass::Get_File creates one for every file it hands
/// out. file_auto_ptr creates one when its factory returns no file. Both
/// use the default constructor. In retail, the constructor that takes a
/// file name has no caller.
///
/// \par Buffer state
/// The read buffer is allocated on the first read that fits in it, and
/// freed again by Close, by most seeks and by the destructor; see
/// BufferedFileClass::Reset_Buffer. Its size comes from a class-wide
/// setting, 16 KB by default, which only derived classes can change. While
/// data is buffered, the underlying file position is ahead of the logical
/// position by the number of buffered bytes that have not been consumed.
///
/// \par Writing
/// Only reads are buffered. Write passes straight to RawFileClass::Write.
/// Its check that no read buffer exists is compiled out of release builds,
/// so in retail a write after a buffered read lands at the underlying
/// position, after the read-ahead data, not at the logical one.
///
/// \see Targa, \ref grp_w1_wwlib_targa_bufffile


/// \fn BufferedFileClass::BufferedFileClass(char const *filename)
/// \brief Creates a buffered file for the given name, with no buffer yet.
///
/// The file name goes to RawFileClass; nothing is opened. No retail BFME 2
/// code calls this constructor.
///
/// \param filename Name of the file, passed to RawFileClass.
/// \see BufferedFileClass::Read


/// \fn BufferedFileClass::~BufferedFileClass(void)
/// \brief Frees the read buffer; RawFileClass's destructor then closes the
///        file.
/// \see BufferedFileClass::Reset_Buffer


/// \fn int BufferedFileClass::Read(void *buffer, int size)
/// \brief Reads up to size bytes, serving them from the read-ahead buffer
///        where possible.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Bytes already buffered are copied out first.
/// - A remainder larger than the buffer size is read directly with
///   RawFileClass::Read, bypassing the buffer.
/// - Otherwise the buffer is allocated if needed (16 KB by default), refilled
///   with one RawFileClass::Read when it is empty, and the remainder is
///   copied from it.
///
/// \param buffer Destination. It is not checked for null.
/// \param size   Number of bytes wanted.
/// \return The number of bytes copied. Less than size means the end of the
///         file was reached.
///
/// \note BFME 2 allocates the buffer with scalar operator new, where Zero
///       Hour used the array form. Reset_Buffer frees it with the matching
///       scalar delete.
/// \see BufferedFileClass::Seek, BufferedFileClass::Reset_Buffer


/// \fn int BufferedFileClass::Seek(int pos, int dir)
/// \brief Moves the file position, consuming buffered data for short
///        forward seeks.
///
/// A forward seek from the current position (SEEK_CUR with pos of 0 or more)
/// keeps the buffer. As many bytes as are buffered are skipped in memory,
/// and only the rest is passed to RawFileClass::Seek. Any other seek frees
/// the buffer and is passed to RawFileClass::Seek unchanged.
///
/// \param pos Offset, relative to dir.
/// \param dir SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR or SEEK_END.
/// \return The new logical position. A seek of 0 from SEEK_CUR returns the
///         current logical position.
///
/// \warning A backward seek from the current position (SEEK_CUR with a
///          negative pos) frees the buffer without first moving the
///          underlying file back to the logical position. It is then
///          applied to the underlying position and lands too far forward by
///          the number of buffered bytes not yet read. SEEK_SET and SEEK_END
///          are not affected.
/// \see BufferedFileClass::Read


/// \fn void BufferedFileClass::Close(void)
/// \brief Closes the file through RawFileClass::Close, then frees the read
///        buffer.
/// \see BufferedFileClass::Reset_Buffer


/// \fn void BufferedFileClass::Reset_Buffer(void)
/// \brief Frees the read buffer, if there is one, and resets the buffer
///        state to empty.
///
/// Discards any buffered bytes not yet read. It does not move the underlying
/// file position; see BufferedFileClass::Seek. In the source, Close, Seek
/// and the destructor call it. In retail all three have it inlined, so the
/// out-of-line copy has no caller.
/// \see BufferedFileClass::Close, BufferedFileClass::Seek
